    {
      "name": "Research.BinaryTreeReduction.roundedProductTerm.eq_1",
      "statement": "For every model m and all x,y in R, roundedProductTerm(m,x,y)=Model.mul(m,x,y). A model m consists of u in R with 0 <= u and a total function r: R -> R such that for every z in R there exists delta in R with |delta| <= u and r(z)=z(1+delta). This identifies the operation applied to every leaf pair by roundedProductTree, and hence the multiplication stage of roundedDotProduct. Combined with the definition of Model.mul, both sides equal r(xy); the statement does not assert equality to 1."
    },
    {
      "name": "Research.BinaryTreeReduction.roundedProductTree_isPerturbation",
      "statement": "For every model m and every t in T(R x R), IsPerturbation(u,exactProductTree(t),roundedProductTree(m,t)). A model m consists of u in R with 0 <= u and a total function r: R -> R such that for every z in R there exists delta in R with |delta| <= u and r(z)=z(1+delta). T(A) denotes finite ordered binary trees generated by leaf(a), a in A, and node(L,R), L,R in T(A); there is no empty tree. For any real bound b, IsPerturbation(b,A,B) is the inductively generated relation: leaf(x) is related to leaf(y) when |y-x| <= b|x|, and node(L,R) is related to node(L_prime,R_prime) when the corresponding children are related with the same b. Thus the target asserts identical ordered tree shape and, at each leaf originally labelled (a,b), |r(ab)-ab| <= u|ab|. The tree shape, number of leaves, and real leaf pairs are arbitrary. It compares the actual exact and rounded product constructions before any of the additions used by roundedDotProduct."
